Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  18 REMOVED_MONTH 97 ACCESSIONED ON  18 REMOVED_MONTH 97 at 13 44 CLINICAL DATA   None Provided                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  LEFT   EXCISIONAL BIOPSY  INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  GRADE 2  REMOVED_DATE CM  EXTENDING TO REMOVED_DATE CM OF THE MARGIN OF THE SPECIMEN  SEE NOTE  NOTE  Blood vessel and lymphatic invasion is not seen  The margins of the specimen appear free of the tumor  Diagnosis by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  20 REMOVED_MONTH 97 at 16 29                                                                                     RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS BREAST  LEFT   The tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein  The tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein  See note  Note  In this laboratory  we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way  paraffin sections  and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay  91  of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  96  of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections  the predictive value of a positive result was 97  and the predictive value of a negative result was 95   In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Sixty five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 98  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  24 REMOVED_MONTH 97 at 15 25 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  left breast mass  is a 3 x REMOVED_DATE x 1cm breast biopsy  Inked and sectioned to reveal a 0 6cm in maximum diameter white gray  well circumscribed nodule within 0 2cm of the closest inked resection margin  The specimen is submitted entirety in cassettes A through I  with nodule in A and B  Resident  DAVID SABLE  MD     INTRA OPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS     GROSS DIAGNOSIS   LEFT BREAST   SUSPICIOUS NODULE  TOO SMALL TO FREEZE  O R  Resident  EDI BROGI  MD  PhD O R  Diagnosis by  Andrew Eric Rosenberg  M D                                                                                  SOURCE CARE UNIT  SAME DAY SURGICAL UNIT    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REPORTS TO  Carlos Fernandez del castillo  M D  PART   BREAST  LEFT   EXCISIONAL BIOPSY   report_end  , Micro.
Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  13 REMOVED_MONTH 93 ACCESSIONED ON  13 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 14 49 CLINICAL DATA  Status post right breast needle localization  abnormal mammogram                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  RIGHT   BIOPSY  INFILTRATING DUCTAL CARCINOMA  GRADE REMOVED_DATE  REMOVED_DATE CM  PRESENT AT AN INKED RESECTION MARGIN  SEE NOTE  NOTE  There is focal extracellular mucin  No definitive vascular or lymphatic space invasion is seen  Diagnosis by  Kathleen K  Hansen  MD Signed On  21 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 14 32                                                                                     S PHASE ANALYSIS Addendum     BREAST   RIGHT  EXCISIONAL BIOPSY   BRDU UPTAKE  PERCENTAGE OF CELLS IN S PHASE   5   SEE NOTE  NOTE  The breast tumor tissue  after being incubated in medium containing a thymidine analog  bromodeoxyuridine  was processed and stained by immunohistochemical methods to detect those cells which were replicating DNA  Positive cells  cells in S phase  were counted by microscopic visualization of the breast tissue  Results from 463 MGH cases of invasive ductal carcinoma have shown cells in S phase ranging from 0  to 40   with a mean 6 5  and median of 5  Benign lesions  and invasive lobular carcinomas have consistantly shown very low counts  less than 3    Added by  Ann D  Thor  M D  Signed On  14 REMOVED_MONTH 94 at 15 34     HORMONE BINDING ASSAY Addendum     RESULTS OF HORMONE BINDING ASSAYS AS REPORTED BY DAVID T  MACLAUGHLIN  PhD   726  REMOVED_CASE_ID   estrogen receptor  44 fm mg cytosolic protein  progesterone receptor  NEGATIVE  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  31 REMOVED_MONTH 94 at 11 35 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  Right breast  is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2 0cm fibrofatty breast tissue fragment with localizing wire and accompanying roentgenogram  This specimen is inked and sectioned to reveal a 1 5cm moderately delineated  tan  rubbery nodule abutting an inked resection margin  Representative section frozen as frozen section  1 and saved frozen  Tissue is sent for ERPR and BRDU  The remainder of the specimen is submitted entirely in cassettes A through F  with the lesion found in A and B  Resident  Timothy R  Quinn  MD     INTRA OPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS     FROZEN SECTION DIAGNOSIS  1  BREAST  RIGHT   EXCISIONAL BIOPSY  CONSISTENT WITH CARCINOMA WITH ATYPICAL FEATURES INCLUDING LOBULAR PATTERN AND QUESTION FOCAL AREAS OF MUCIN PRODUCTION  O R  Resident  Jacqueline Haas  MD O R  Diagnosis by  Austin L  Vickery  M D                                                                                  SOURCE CARE UNIT  SURGICAL DAY CARE   ACC 3 REPORTS TO  Wiley Souba  MD PART   BREAST  RIGHT   BIOPSY   report_end  , Micro.
Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Updated Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report  MS 08  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  CASE  MS 08  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  Date Taken  REMOVED_DATE Source Care Unit  Same Day Surgery  WACC 3 Path Subspecialty Service  Breast 1 Results To  MICHELE ANNETTE GADD  M D  Resident  Anna McDonald  M D  Signed Out by  Ronald Balassanian  M D             Revised Report            Reason for Amendment  1  Corrected Final Diagnosis Reason for Addendum  1  Additional Immunohistochemistry Results Reason for Amendment  2  Corrected Transcription Error not in Final Diagnosis CLINICAL DATA  The patient is a 60 year old woman with left breast enhancement seen on MRI  A prior core biopsy demonstrated probable lobular neoplasia  FINAL DIAGNOSIS  BREAST  LEFT   EXCISION  1  DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  2  EXTENSIVE LOBULAR NEOPLASIA  LOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU  WITH CALCIFICATIONS   SEE TABLE 1 AND NOTE   TABLE OF PATHOLOGICAL FINDINGS  1 DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  DCIS  TUMOR SIZE  Involving 4 of 6 blocks  GRADE  3  CALCIFICATIONS  Limited to the carcinoma in situ  Both LCIS and DCIS   MARGIN OF DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  Positive along the inked margin of the specimen along a broad front  STAINS FOR RECEPTORS  Requested on block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   NOTE  Sections of the excision sample reveal large ducts distended by tumor cells  In many areas the tumor cells are large and pleomorphic with abundant cytoplasm and occasional single mucin vacuoles  A few cells have a signet ring appearance  The nuclei are mildly enlarged with mildly irregular contours and granular chromatin  The morphologic findings in these areas are compatible with pleomorphic variant of lobular carcinoma in situ  Intermixed with these areas are other ducts with cells that have a similar appearance but with a distinctly cribriform pattern compatible with a component of DCIS  An immunohistochemical stain for E cadherin on Slide  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  is largely negative and supports the diagnosis of LCIS  however  there is weak membrane staining in the ductal cells in the areas of cribriform architecture supporting a diagnosis DCIS  Stains for p63  myosin  calponin and keratin performed on slides  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  do not show evidence of an invasive carcinoma  Overall  the morphologic findings are compatible with carcinoma in situ with both ductal and lobular features  Immunostains for estrogen receptor and progesterone receptor will be reported in an addendum  This case was amended to correct the final diagnosis  On initial review  the findings were interpreted as extensive LCIS  However  upon review of the e cadherin as well as the ER  PR and Her 2neu stain the findings were interpreted as both ductal and lobular carcinoma in situ  The final diagnosis and note in the report has been revised to reflect these changes  By his her signature below  the pathologist listed as making the Final Diagnosis certifies that he she has personally reviewed this case and confirmed or corrected the diagnoses  GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  Donovan  REMOVE_MRN and  left breast needle loc   is an unoriented portion of fibroadipose tissue  5 0 x REMOVED_DATE x 0 6   with two needle localization wires in place  The specimen is entirely inked black and serially sectioned to reveal no mass lesions  The specimen is entirely included in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  with   prior biopsy site in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   ADDENDUM NOTE  REASON  1 QUANTITATIVE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR RECEPTOR PROTEINS Breast  Left  block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    The ductal carcinoma in situ tumor cells do not stain for estrogen receptor protein  The ductal carcinoma in situ tumor cells do not stain for progesterone receptor protein  The ductal carcinoma in situ tumor cells show strong  3   overexpression of HER 2 c erbB 2  See note  Note  Estrogen and progesterone receptor quantitation  The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the tumor cells is assessed visually and reported as follows  The tumor cells stain    10  of nuclei stained  Some tumor cells stain  1 to  10  of nuclei stained  Rare tumor cells stain    1  of nuclei stained  The tumor cells do not stain  0  of nuclei stained  HER 2 c erbB 2 quantitation  the tumor cells membrane immunoreactivity is evaluated visually and reported as follows  Do not show overexpression  0 to 1   no minimal immunoreactivity or incomplete membrane staining of tumor cells  Show moderate  2   overexpression  weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in   10  of tumor cells  Show strong  3   overexpression  strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in   10  of tumor cells  These tests were developed and their performance characteristics determined by the Pathology Department at the Massachusetts General Hospital  They have not been cleared or approved by the U S  Food and Drug Administration  FDA   The FDA has determined that such clearance or approval is not necessary  this laboratory has established and verified the test s accuracy and precision  Amended report This report was amended to correct the acronym in the title of the table of pathologic findings  The acronym previously listed as  LCIS  has been changed to  DCIS   Final Diagnosis by Ronald Balassanian M D   on REMOVED_DATE Amendment  1 by Ronald Balassanian M D   on REMOVED_DATE Addendum  1 by Atul K Bhan M D   on REMOVED_DATE Amendment  2 by Ronald Balassanian M D   Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE PART   BREAST  LEFT   EXCISION  report_end  , Micro.
Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  08 REMOVED_MONTH 95 ACCESSIONED ON  08 REMOVED_MONTH 95 at 14 24 CLINICAL DATA   None Provided                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  LEFT   EXCISION  INFILTRATING DUCTAL CARCINOMA  GRADE I  FORMING TWO NODULES REMOVED_DATE CM AND REMOVED_DATE CM IN GREATEST DIMENSION  AND EXTENDING TO THE MARGIN OF THE SPECIMEN  DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU CONFINED TO THE REGION OF THE MASS  SEE NOTE  NOTE  The two nodules appear identical histologically and therefore probably represent independent foci of invasion originating from a single neoplastic population  Blood vessel and lymphatic vessel invasion is not seen  LYMPH NODES  LEFT AXILLA   THERE IS NO EVIDENCE OF MALIGNANCY IN 13 OF 13 LYMPH NODES  Diagnosis by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  13 REMOVED_MONTH 95 at 15 12                                                                                     RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS BREAST  LEFT   The tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein  The tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein  See note  Note  In this laboratory  we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way  paraffin sections  and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay  91  of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  96  of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections  the predictive value of a positive result was 97  and the predictive value of a negative result was 95   In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Sixty five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 98  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  THE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR PROTEIN ARE FOR INVESTIGATIONAL USE ONLY  THEY MUST NOT BE USED FOR CLINICAL PURPOSES  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  18 REMOVED_MONTH 95 at 16 13 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh  in the Frozen Section Lab   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name and unit number only  is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2 0cm fragment of breast with an indistinct firm area and a localizing wire in place   No accompanying radiograph   Sectioned to reveal a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 0cm irregular tan firm lesion  extending to within 0 5cm of surface ink  There is adjacent parenchymal hemorrhage  Sections of mass in A through D  with other representative sections in E through J  Received labeled  left axillary nodes  is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 5cm fragment of fatty tissue containing multiple tan rubbery lymph node as large as 1 1cm  submitted in cassettes K through N  Resident  MARYAM S  MOHAMMADKHANI  MD                                                                                 SOURCE CARE UNIT  SAME DAY SURGICAL UNIT    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REPORTS TO  Wiley W Souba Jr   M D  PART A  LEFT BREAST EXCISION PART B  LEFT AXILLARY LYMPH NODES   report_end  , Micro.
